    public static final List f1645a = u1.h.F(new a1.i(1, "What is HARIS PRO?", "HARIS PRO is an HR Navigator app that helps companies build, audit, and improve their human resources systems. It combines practical tools, diagnostics, and expert guidance across recruitment, onboarding, performance management, and culture. The app is designed for HR professionals, managers, and business owners who want structured HR without enterprise complexity."), new a1.i(2, "Who is HARIS PRO designed for?", "HARIS PRO serves HR managers, team leads, founders, and growing companies that need professional HR practices but lack a full HR department. It works equally well for startups establishing their first processes and mid-size firms refining existing systems. No prior HR certification is required to use the app effectively."), new a1.i(3, "What HR areas does HARIS PRO cover?", "The app covers recruitment, onboarding, KPIs, motivation, corporate culture, HR analytics, grades, competencies, training, leadership, regulations, job descriptions, performance reviews, one-to-one meetings, employer branding, employee journey mapping, exit interviews, and organizational structure. Each area includes articles, checklists, and actionable recommendations."), new a1.i(4, "How does the HR diagnostic tool work?", "The diagnostic asks 20 questions about your current HR maturity across policies, onboarding, metrics, and culture. Your answers produce a score and a maturity level: High, Medium, or Low. Based on the result, you receive targeted recommendations and improvement priorities tailored to your gaps."), new a1.i(5, "Is HARIS PRO a replacement for an HR department?", "No. HARIS PRO is a navigator and toolkit, not a substitute for human judgment or legal counsel. It helps you design processes, identify gaps, and apply best practices faster. For complex labor disputes, mergers, or jurisdiction-specific compliance, you should still consult qualified HR or legal professionals."), new a1.i(6, "What is the difference between KPIs and competencies?", "KPIs measure outcomes — what was achieved, such as revenue per employee or time-to-hire. Competencies describe behaviors and skills — how work is done, such as communication or problem-solving. HARIS PRO treats both as complementary: KPIs track results, competencies define the capabilities needed to deliver them."), new a1.i(7, "How long should onboarding last?", "Effective onboarding typically spans 30 to 90 days, not just the first week. The first month focuses on tools, role clarity, and cultural integration; days 31–90 shift toward independent contribution and feedback. HARIS PRO recommends structuring onboarding in phases with clear milestones and check-ins at each stage."), new a1.i(8, "Why is a one-to-one meeting important?", "Regular one-to-one meetings build trust, surface blockers early, and keep goals aligned between manager and employee. They are not status updates — they are dedicated conversations about development, wellbeing, and priorities. HARIS PRO recommends weekly or biweekly sessions of 30–45 minutes with a consistent agenda."), new a1.i(9, "What is an employee journey map?", "An employee journey map visualizes every touchpoint an employee experiences from first contact as a candidate through exit. It covers recruitment, onboarding, daily work, development, recognition, and offboarding. Mapping the journey helps identify friction points that cause disengagement or turnover."), new a1.i(10, "How often should performance reviews be conducted?", "Most organizations benefit from a formal review once or twice per year, supplemented by ongoing feedback and quarterly check-ins. Annual-only reviews create recency bias and surprise employees with ratings they did not expect. HARIS PRO advocates continuous feedback with a structured summary at defined intervals."), new a1.i(11, "What is employer branding and why does it matter?", "Employer branding is how your company is perceived as a place to work, both internally and externally. A strong HR brand reduces cost-per-hire, attracts better candidates, and improves retention. It is built through consistent messaging, employee experience, and authentic stories — not just marketing slogans."), new a1.i(12, "What should an exit interview cover?", "Exit interviews should explore reasons for leaving, manager relationship, growth opportunities, compensation perception, and culture fit. The goal is to gather honest feedback without creating conflict. HARIS PRO recommends a structured questionnaire conducted by HR or a neutral third party, not the direct manager."), new a1.i(13, "How do I build a grade system for my company?", "Start by grouping roles into job families, then define levels within each family based on scope, impact, and required experience. Each grade should have clear criteria for promotion and a corresponding compensation band. Avoid creating too many levels — five to seven grades per family is usually sufficient."), new a1.i(14, "What HR metrics should I track first?", "Begin with turnover rate, time-to-hire, cost-per-hire, and employee engagement score. These four metrics reveal whether you are losing people, hiring efficiently, and keeping teams motivated. HARIS PRO's analytics section explains how to calculate each metric and set realistic benchmarks for your industry."), new a1.i(15, "What is a competency model?", "A competency model defines the knowledge, skills, and behaviors required for success in specific roles or across the organization. It standardizes hiring criteria, training priorities, and promotion decisions. HARIS PRO recommends starting with 5–8 core competencies for the whole company, then adding role-specific ones."), new a1.i(16, "How can I improve corporate culture without a big budget?", "Culture is shaped by daily behaviors, not expensive perks. Start with transparent communication, recognition rituals, consistent management standards, and involving employees in decisions that affect them. Measure culture through pulse surveys and act on the results visibly — employees notice when feedback leads to change."), new a1.i(17, "What makes a good job description?", "A strong job description clearly states the role purpose, key responsibilities, required competencies, reporting line, and success criteria. It should attract the right candidates without overstating or understating the role. HARIS PRO recommends reviewing and updating descriptions at least once a year or whenever the role changes."), new a1.i(18, "How do I motivate employees beyond salary?", "Non-financial motivators include growth opportunities, autonomy, meaningful work, recognition, and a sense of belonging. Different people are driven by different factors — ask your team what matters to them. HARIS PRO's motivation tools help you design a balanced reward mix tailored to your workforce."), new a1.i(19, "What is the role of HR in organizational structure design?", "HR facilitates structure design by mapping current roles, identifying overlaps and gaps, and ensuring the structure supports business strategy. The goal is clarity: everyone should know who they report to, who they collaborate with, and what decisions they own. Structure should be reviewed when the company grows or pivots."), new a1.i(20, "How do I run an effective recruitment process?", "Define the role clearly, create a structured interview pipeline, use consistent evaluation criteria, and communicate timelines to candidates. Reduce bias by having multiple interviewers and scoring candidates against competencies, not gut feeling. HARIS PRO provides recruitment checklists and templates to standardize each stage."), new a1.i(21, "What training should every manager receive?", "Managers need training in giving feedback, conducting one-to-ones, setting goals, handling conflict, and basic labor regulations. Technical expertise alone does not make someone an effective people manager. HARIS PRO recommends a manager onboarding program within the first 30 days of a promotion."), new a1.i(22, "What are typical HR compliance documents?", "Core documents include an employee handbook, employment contracts, job descriptions, leave policies, code of conduct, and data protection policies. Requirements vary by jurisdiction, so always verify local labor law. HARIS PRO helps you understand what each document should contain and how to keep them current."), new a1.i(23, "How does HARIS PRO help with leadership development?", "The app provides articles, diagnostics, and frameworks for building leadership pipelines, succession planning, and manager coaching. It covers both strategic leadership competencies and practical skills like delegation and decision-making. Use the leadership section to assess gaps and create development plans for your management team."), new a1.i(24, "Can I use HARIS PRO checklists in my daily work?", "Yes. HARIS PRO includes ready-made checklists for onboarding, recruitment, performance reviews, exit interviews, and more. You can follow them step by step or adapt them to your company's processes. Checklists ensure consistency and reduce the chance of missing critical steps."), new a1.i(25, "What is the ROI of investing in HR processes?", "Companies with mature HR practices see lower turnover, faster hiring, higher productivity, and fewer legal risks. While ROI is hard to measure precisely, reducing one bad hire or one wrongful termination case often pays for years of HR investment. HARIS PRO helps you prioritize changes with the highest impact."), new a1.i(26, "How do I handle underperformance?", "Address underperformance early with specific examples, clear expectations, and a documented improvement plan. Give the employee support and a reasonable timeframe to improve. If performance does not change, follow your company's disciplinary process consistently and fairly."), new a1.i(27, "What is the difference between a performance review and a one-to-one?", "A one-to-one is an ongoing conversation about work, development, and support. A performance review is a formal evaluation against goals and competencies, usually tied to compensation or promotion decisions. Both are needed, but they serve different purposes and should not be merged into a single meeting."), new a1.i(28, "How do I create a career path for employees?", "Map out possible progression routes within each job family — vertical (promotion) and horizontal (lateral moves). Define what skills and results are needed to advance to each level. Share these paths openly so employees know how to grow rather than guessing or leaving for opportunities elsewhere."), new a1.i(29, "What is HR analytics and do I need special software?", "HR analytics is the practice of collecting and interpreting people data to make better workforce decisions. You can start with a spreadsheet tracking turnover, hiring speed, and survey scores. HARIS PRO explains key formulas and helps you interpret trends before you invest in dedicated HRIS platforms."), new a1.i(30, "How should I structure a training program?", "Identify skill gaps through performance data and manager input, then prioritize training by business impact. Mix formats: onboarding sessions, workshops, mentoring, and self-paced learning. Measure effectiveness by observing behavior change and performance improvement, not just attendance."), new a1.i(31, "What is succession planning?", "Succession planning identifies and develops internal candidates for critical roles before they become vacant. It reduces disruption when key people leave and demonstrates growth opportunities to the wider team. Start with your top 5–10 critical positions and assess whether ready successors exist."), new a1.i(32, "How do I reduce employee turnover?", "Analyze exit interview data to find patterns — common reasons include poor management, lack of growth, and compensation gaps. Address root causes systematically rather than relying on retention bonuses alone. Regular engagement surveys help you catch dissatisfaction before people start looking elsewhere."), new a1.i(33, "What is a 360-degree feedback review?", "A 360-degree review collects feedback from an employee's manager, peers, direct reports, and sometimes clients. It provides a multi-perspective view of strengths and development areas. Use it for development purposes, not as the sole input for compensation decisions, to encourage honest feedback."), new a1.i(34, "How does HARIS PRO handle data privacy?", "HARIS PRO stores diagnostic and tool data locally on your device. It does not transmit personal employee data to external servers. You are responsible for handling any employee information you enter according to your local data protection regulations, such as GDPR or equivalent laws."), new a1.i(35, "Can HARIS PRO be used in companies with remote teams?", "Yes. Many HARIS PRO tools — one-to-one frameworks, onboarding checklists, performance review templates, and engagement practices — are designed to work regardless of location. Remote teams often benefit even more from structured HR processes because informal office interactions are less available."), new a1.i(36, "What is the best way to introduce HR changes to a resistant team?", "Explain the why behind each change, involve employees in the design where possible, and pilot before rolling out company-wide. Resistance often comes from fear of extra bureaucracy, not opposition to improvement. Start with one visible quick win to build credibility for larger initiatives."), new a1.i(37, "How do I align individual goals with company strategy?", "Cascade company objectives into department and individual goals using the OKR or KPI framework. Each employee should see a clear line from their daily work to organizational priorities. Review alignment during one-to-ones and performance reviews to keep goals current as strategy evolves."), new a1.i(38, "What is the difference between HR operations and HR strategy?", "HR operations covers day-to-day tasks: hiring, payroll, policies, and employee requests. HR strategy focuses on workforce planning, culture, leadership development, and aligning people practices with business goals. HARIS PRO helps you balance both — fixing operational gaps while building long-term capability."), new a1.i(39, "How often should I update HR policies?", "Review all policies at least annually and update them whenever labor laws change, the company grows significantly, or new work models are introduced. Outdated policies create legal risk and employee confusion. Communicate changes clearly and collect acknowledgment from affected employees."), new a1.i(40, "Where can I get help if I am stuck on an HR topic in the app?", "Start with the relevant article in the Knowledge Base — each covers what the topic is, why it matters, common mistakes, and recommendations. Use the diagnostic tool to identify your weakest areas and prioritize accordingly. For topics not covered in the app, consult a qualified HR or legal advisor in your jurisdiction."), new a1.i(41, "What is a healthy employee turnover rate?", "Most industries consider 10-15% annual turnover healthy. Some turnover is natural and beneficial — it brings fresh perspectives. Concern arises above 20%, especially if top performers are leaving. Track voluntary vs involuntary turnover separately for accurate diagnosis."), new a1.i(42, "How do I calculate cost per hire?", "Add all recruitment costs (job ads, agency fees, interviewer time, background checks, onboarding setup) and divide by the number of hires in the period. Most companies underestimate this by 30-50% by excluding internal time costs."), new a1.i(43, "When should a startup hire its first HR person?", "Generally between 15-25 employees, or when the founder spends more than 20% of time on people issues. Before that, use HR templates, checklists, and outsourced HR support from the app and consultants."), new a1.i(44, "What is the difference between KPIs and OKRs?", "KPIs measure ongoing health of business processes (turnover rate, time-to-fill). OKRs are ambitious goals with key results for a specific period (quarterly). Use KPIs for monitoring, OKRs for driving change. Many companies use both."), new a1.i(45, "How often should performance reviews happen?", "Formal reviews twice a year with quarterly check-ins is the modern standard. Annual-only reviews are outdated — employees need regular feedback to improve. Never let more than 6 months pass without a structured conversation about performance."), new a1.i(46, "What should be in an employee handbook?", "Company overview, code of conduct, leave policies, working hours, compensation overview, benefits, anti-harassment policy, disciplinary procedures, health and safety, data protection, and grievance process. Keep language clear and accessible."), new a1.i(47, "How do I handle a toxic manager?", "Document specific behaviors and impact. Gather 360-feedback data. Have a direct conversation with HR and senior leadership. If coaching fails, act decisively — toxic managers cause disproportionate team turnover and legal risk."), new a1.i(48, "What is compa-ratio and why does it matter?", "Compa-ratio is employee salary divided by the midpoint of their pay range. A ratio of 1.0 means at market midpoint. Below 0.85 signals underpayment risk; above 1.15 may indicate overpayment or grade misassignment."), new a1.i(49, "How do I improve offer acceptance rate?", "Speed up the process, be transparent about compensation early, sell the role and culture during interviews, and follow up personally after extending the offer. Candidates who wait more than a week for an offer are 50% more likely to decline."), new a1.i(50, "What are stay interviews?", "Proactive conversations with current employees to understand what keeps them and what might make them leave. Unlike exit interviews, stay interviews prevent departures by addressing concerns while the employee is still engaged."), new a1.i(51, "How do I build a competency framework?", "Identify 5-8 core competencies for your organization. Define behavioral indicators for each level (junior, mid, senior). Map competencies to roles. Use them for hiring scorecards, performance reviews, and development planning."), new a1.i(52, "What is the 9-box grid?", "A talent assessment tool plotting employees on performance (x-axis) and potential (y-axis). It identifies stars, solid performers, and development needs. Used for succession planning and targeted investment in high-potential employees."), new a1.i(53, "How do I measure training ROI?", "Track pre and post-training performance metrics, completion rates, manager observations, and application of skills on the job. Not all training has immediate ROI — leadership development pays off over 12-18 months."), new a1.i(54, "What is psychological safety?", "The belief that you can speak up, ask questions, admit mistakes, and propose ideas without fear of punishment or humiliation. Google's research found it is the #1 factor in high-performing teams. Built through leader behavior, not policies."), new a1.i(55, "How do I use the HR Metrics Guide in this app?", "Go to Tools → HR Metrics Guide to browse 25 essential HR metrics with formulas, industry benchmarks, and HARIS PRO tips. Use them to build your HR dashboard and track what matters for your business."));
